RFC-20260930-agentjob: AgentJob — a Michelangelo CRD for Sandboxed, Run-to-Completion Agent Execution
Status: Draft
Author(s): @TODO-nolan-github-handle
Created: 2026-09-30
Internal ERD: (internal, not linked here)
Problem statement
Michelangelo has first-class workload CRDs for batch/ML compute (Job, RayJob, SparkJob in proto/api/v2), each scheduling a well-defined, trusted workload with a known resource shape and a fixed entrypoint, launched (in the common case) from a Uniflow-compiled PipelineRun workflow via a worker activity (go/worker/activities/*) that calls the CRD's gRPC service.

None of these fit a growing class of workload: an autonomous agent harness that runs arbitrary, often LLM-generated, code or shell commands to completion inside a single execution (for example: an agent that plans, writes, and runs code to answer a task, then exits). This workload differs from existing job types in ways that matter for both its API shape and its security model:

Untrusted/semi-trusted execution. The code path executed is not fully known ahead of time, so it needs strong isolation (syscall filtering, no host network by default, no persistent state) beyond what a normal batch/v1.Job pod or a RayJob's worker pod gets.
Single-run semantics with a richer failure taxonomy. An agent run is not a long-lived service and not a retryable batch step — it runs once, to a terminal state, similar in spirit to how a RayJob reaches SUCCEEDED/FAILED. But "the sandbox never came up" and "the agent's own generated code failed" are qualitatively different events that existing job CRDs' binary succeeded/failed status can't distinguish, and that distinction is exactly the debugging signal users of a sandboxed-execution CRD most need.
A payload, not a script reference, plus a structured result, not just a log stream to scrape.
Today, teams that need this either bypass Michelangelo entirely (hand-rolled Kubernetes Jobs with no scheduling/resource-pool/observability integration) or overload an existing CRD. We propose AgentJob, a new Michelangelo CRD purpose-built for this workload, whose execution isolation is delegated to kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox rather than reimplemented.

Scope boundary: why a standalone CRD, not a pipeline-task plugin
Michelangelo already has a lighter-weight pattern for adding a new compute backend to a pipeline: a Uniflow task plugin that owns its resource directly within a PipelineRun's workflow (the RayTask-style pattern), without a dedicated top-level CRD. We considered this for AgentJob and rejected it (see Alternatives, option D) because agent-harness runs are frequently submitted and polled independently of any pipeline — by an external caller, a CLI, or a service — and need their own get/apply/delete/list lifecycle, RBAC, and audit trail the same way RayJob does. A task-plugin model would make AgentJob invisible outside the pipeline that launched it, which defeats a core goal below.

Motivation
Give agent-harness workloads the same first-class citizenship as RayJob/SparkJob: submission via the Michelangelo API, phase/status tracking, resource-pool-aware scheduling, and consistent RBAC/audit through UserInfo.
Avoid reinventing pod-level isolation. kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox already provides Sandbox/SandboxClaim/SandboxWarmPool primitives with gVisor-based isolation (Kata/Firecracker also supported via RuntimeClass) — exactly the isolation properties an agent-harness workload needs. No OSS job-CRD project we surveyed reimplements this kind of isolation once a dedicated isolation CRD exists in the ecosystem; AgentJob should consume agent-sandbox, not duplicate it.
Reuse Michelangelo's existing, already-public scheduling/placement vocabulary (Affinity, TerminationSpec, SchedulingSpec, AssignmentInfo, SchedulingInfo in proto/api/v2/job.proto) instead of introducing a parallel set of concepts specific to agent workloads.
Give AgentJob's status model a 3-way terminal split and a machine-readable failure reason, not the coarser 2-way success/fail that RayJob/SparkJob use today — every OSS run-to-completion job CRD we surveyed (Argo's Failed vs Error, RayJob's JobFailedReason, Flyte's distinct Aborted) has converged on this, and it matters more here because "sandbox never provisioned" vs. "agent code exited non-zero" vs. "we killed it" are fundamentally different operator actions.
Goals
Define an AgentJob CRD (proto/api/v2/agent_job.proto) with AgentJobSpec / AgentJobStatus / AgentJobPhase, following the same structural conventions as RayJob/SparkJob (TypeMeta/ObjectMeta/Spec/Status, Condition-based sub-status, explicit _INVALID = 0 enum defaults).
Specify how AgentJob's controller creates and manages an underlying kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox Sandbox/SandboxClaim as the execution primitive for a single run, reacting to its status via watches (Owns()/Watches()), not polling — explicitly avoiding the RayJob controller's known gap (go/components/ray/job/controller.go, tracked as TODO(#605)) of polling a foreign CRD's status on a flat interval, which is unnecessary here since Sandbox/SandboxClaim live in the same cluster (unlike Ray's federated remote-cluster jobs).
Guarantee cleanup of the Sandbox/SandboxClaim on AgentJob deletion — a leaked sandbox is leaked, potentially security-sensitive compute, and the RayJob controller does not clearly solve this for its own foreign resource today; AgentJob must close this gap rather than inherit it.
Support passing a run-specific payload to the sandboxed execution, and produce a structured result (exit code, bounded output, optional artifacts) rather than requiring consumers to scrape logs.
Distinguish infra-level failure (sandbox never provisioned) from agent-level failure (harness ran but the agent's own task failed) via a phase/reason model, and model retries as always claiming a fresh Sandbox/SandboxClaim, never re-entering one that already ran semi-trusted code — a security requirement, not just a hygiene one.
Reuse existing Michelangelo API types (UserInfo, Affinity, TerminationSpec, SchedulingSpec, ClusterSpec) wherever their semantics already match.
Define a minimal, generic AgentJobResources shape (CPU/memory/GPU requests) independent of any one caller's runtime.
Non-goals
Re-implementing or extending kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox itself (its Sandbox, SandboxClaim, SandboxWarmPool, and isolation model are treated as an external dependency; see [[082]] for a separate, upstream-facing proposal about hardening/operating that project, out of scope here).
Multi-step or long-running agent orchestration (planning graphs, multi-turn conversations across separate AgentJob runs) — AgentJob models exactly one bounded execution, analogous to a single RayJob/SparkJob run. Composing multiple AgentJobs into a higher-level workflow is left to existing Michelangelo pipeline/DAG tooling (a new go/worker/activities/agent + workflows/agent pair can invoke AgentJob from a Uniflow-compiled pipeline the same way RayJob/SparkJob are invoked today, but that pair is not itself part of this proposal).
A general-purpose "run arbitrary container" API — AgentJob is scoped to the agent-harness use case (payload in, isolated execution, structured result out), not a replacement for batch/v1.Job or RayJob.
Autoscaling or fleet-wide warm-pool sizing policy for the underlying sandbox pool — deferred to whatever guidance kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox itself provides for SandboxWarmPool sizing.
A run/logs/watch CLI verb beyond the standard get/apply/delete (see Open Questions) — worth deciding explicitly rather than silently inheriting RayJob/SparkJob's current gap here, but not blocking the initial CRD/controller proposal in this RFC.
High-level architecture
                     +-------------------------+
   AgentJob (CRD) -->|  go/components/agentjob  |
   (this proposal)   |  controller              |
                     +------------+-------------+
                                  |
                                  | Owns()/Watches() (same-cluster,
                                  | not polled — see Goals)
                                  v
                     +-------------------------+
                     |  Sandbox / SandboxClaim  |   <- kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox
                     |  (gVisor-isolated pod)   |      (external dependency, unmodified,
                     +------------+-------------+       vendored/pinned Go API types)
                                  |
                                  | exec API: run harness w/ payload
                                  v
                     +-------------------------+
                     |  Agent harness process   |
                     |  (image from spec.image) |
                     +-------------------------+
AgentJob's controller is a new package, go/components/agentjob/{job,module.go}, registered onto the shared controller-runtime Manager via fx.Invoke(register) exactly like go/components/ray/job/module.go — not a fork of agent-sandbox's own controller.
On creation, the controller resolves scheduling/placement (Affinity, ClusterSpec) via the existing jobs/scheduler (Kueue) + jobs/cluster + jobs/client federation layer, extending jobs/compute.Factory with an "agent sandbox" backend — the same extension point RayJob/SparkJob already use for their own compute engines.
The controller creates a Sandbox (or claims one from a SandboxWarmPool via SandboxClaim), Owns()/Watches() it so status changes trigger immediate reconciles, and invokes the sandbox's exec API to start the agent harness (spec.image) with the run's payload.
Cleanup contract (explicit, not implicit): the AgentJob controller either (a) sets a Kubernetes owner reference from the Sandbox/SandboxClaim to the AgentJob so garbage collection deletes it automatically, or (b) uses an explicit finalizer on AgentJob that deletes the Sandbox/SandboxClaim before allowing the AgentJob CR itself to be removed. This RFC proposes (a) as the default (simpler, matches how most Kubernetes controllers manage owned resources) with (b) as a fallback if agent-sandbox resources don't support owner references from a foreign apiGroup cleanly — see Open Questions.
On terminal state (success, failure, infra error, or an explicit kill request), the controller tears down the sandbox (or returns it to its warm pool, if agent-sandbox supports that for a used claim — likely not, given the "never reuse a sandbox that ran untrusted code" requirement above) and records status (see APIs section) mirroring how SparkJobStatus/RayJobStatus do today, following the same "mark immutable → ingester archives to MySQL → CR deleted from etcd" lifecycle every other terminal Michelangelo CRD uses.
Runtime variants
Two integration patterns are anticipated (see also the more detailed discussion of this trade-off in [[082]]'s upstream-facing draft, from the sandbox-operator side):

Harness runs inside the sandbox. The sandbox image itself bundles the agent harness; AgentJob's controller execs a single command and waits for exit. Simpler, maps directly onto agent-sandbox's existing exec-and-wait pattern.
External harness drives the sandbox via its exec API. The controller (or a controller-managed sidecar) treats the sandbox purely as an isolated execution surface and drives it step-by-step. More flexible, more moving parts.
This RFC proposes starting with (1), and leaves (2) as a documented future extension point rather than building it now.

APIs and CRDs
New file proto/api/v2/agent_job.proto, following the existing spark_job.proto/ray_job.proto layout and conventions (TypeMeta/ObjectMeta embedding, option (michelangelo.api.resource) = { conversion: true };, option (michelangelo.api.resource_list) = {};, explicit _INVALID = 0 enum defaults, extensive field-level doc comments as the documentation source of truth):

syntax = "proto3";

package michelangelo.api.v2;

option go_package = "v2";

import "google/protobuf/struct.proto";
import "k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/apis/meta/v1/generated.proto";
import "michelangelo/api/options.proto";
import "michelangelo/api/conditions.proto";
import "michelangelo/api/v2/job.proto";
import "michelangelo/api/v2/user.proto";
import "michelangelo/api/v2/cluster.proto";

// AgentJobPhase enumerates the lifecycle states of an AgentJob run.
// Distinguishes infra-level outcomes (sandbox never came up, killed by us)
// from agent-level outcomes (harness ran, agent's own task succeeded/failed),
// per the 3-way terminal split used by Argo Workflows (Succeeded/Failed/Error)
// and RayJob's infra/app status split.
enum AgentJobPhase {
  AGENT_JOB_PHASE_INVALID = 0;
  AGENT_JOB_PHASE_PENDING = 1;       // not yet scheduled
  AGENT_JOB_PHASE_PROVISIONING = 2;  // Sandbox/SandboxClaim requested, not yet ready
  AGENT_JOB_PHASE_RUNNING = 3;       // sandbox ready, harness executing
  AGENT_JOB_PHASE_SUCCEEDED = 4;     // agent's own task completed successfully
  AGENT_JOB_PHASE_FAILED = 5;        // harness ran; agent's task failed (non-zero exit, policy violation)
  AGENT_JOB_PHASE_ERRORED = 6;       // infra-level failure: sandbox never provisioned, node eviction, etc.
  AGENT_JOB_PHASE_KILLING = 7;       // termination requested, sandbox teardown in progress
  AGENT_JOB_PHASE_KILLED = 8;
}

// Machine-readable reason accompanying a terminal AgentJobPhase, analogous to
// RayJob's JobFailedReason. `message` (see AgentJobStatus) remains the
// free-text human-readable string; `reason` is the stable, matchable enum.
enum AgentJobFailureReason {
  AGENT_JOB_FAILURE_REASON_INVALID = 0;
  AGENT_JOB_FAILURE_REASON_SANDBOX_PROVISION_FAILED = 1;
  AGENT_JOB_FAILURE_REASON_SANDBOX_CLAIM_TIMEOUT = 2;
  AGENT_JOB_FAILURE_REASON_AGENT_TIMEOUT = 3;
  AGENT_JOB_FAILURE_REASON_AGENT_NON_ZERO_EXIT = 4;
  AGENT_JOB_FAILURE_REASON_AGENT_POLICY_VIOLATION = 5;
  AGENT_JOB_FAILURE_REASON_AGENT_OOM_KILLED = 6;
}

// Reference to the agent definition/harness image to execute.
message AgentDefinitionRef {
  // Container image reference for the agent harness.
  string image = 1;
}

// Resource requests for the sandboxed execution.
message AgentJobResources {
  string cpu = 1;
  string memory = 2;
  // Optional GPU resource name (e.g. "nvidia.com/gpu") and count.
  string gpu_type = 3;
  int32 gpu_count = 4;
}

// Structured result of a completed run, avoiding the need to scrape sandbox
// logs for the answer (cf. Argo's NodeStatus.Outputs.Result pattern).
message AgentJobResult {
  int32 exit_code = 1;
  // Bounded, truncated summary of the harness's own reported output.
  string output = 2;
  // Opaque, caller-defined structured result payload.
  google.protobuf.Struct artifacts = 3;
}

message AgentJobSpec {
  // User initiating the agent run.
  UserInfo user = 1 [(michelangelo.api.validation) = {required: true}];

  // Placement/resource-selection rules — reused as-is from job.proto.
  Affinity affinity = 2;

  // Preemption/scheduling behavior — reused as-is from job.proto.
  SchedulingSpec scheduling = 3;

  // Target cluster for the underlying sandbox.
  ClusterSpec cluster = 4;

  // Caller-supplied identifier correlating this run to an external system.
  // Opaque to Michelangelo; not interpreted or validated beyond length/charset.
  string run_id = 5 [(michelangelo.api.validation) = {
    required: true, max_length: "256", pattern: "^[A-Za-z0-9._:-]+$"
  }];

  // Agent harness image to run inside the sandbox.
  AgentDefinitionRef agent_definition = 6;

  // Run-specific input payload passed to the harness. Marked sensitive since
  // this may carry caller-provided task input.
  google.protobuf.Struct payload = 7 [(michelangelo.api.sensitive) = true];

  // Resource requests for the sandbox.
  AgentJobResources resources = 8;

  // Deadline for the underlying Sandbox/SandboxClaim to become ready, bounding
  // provisioning time (cold pod / cold microVM boot, warm-pool exhaustion)
  // separately from total run time — mirrors RayJob's preRunningDeadlineSeconds.
  int32 sandbox_ready_deadline_seconds = 9;

  // Deadline for the whole run (provisioning + execution) — mirrors k8s Job's
  // and RayJob's activeDeadlineSeconds.
  int32 active_deadline_seconds = 10;

  // How long to retain a terminal AgentJob record before cleanup. Callers may
  // want a shorter TTL for Failed/Errored runs than Succeeded ones, given a
  // failed sandbox may hold less-trustworthy artifacts/state.
  int32 ttl_seconds_after_finished = 11;
}

message AgentJobStatus {
  AgentJobPhase phase = 1;

  // Machine-readable failure reason, set when phase is FAILED or ERRORED.
  AgentJobFailureReason failure_reason = 2;

  // Human-readable status message.
  string message = 3;

  // Where the run was placed.
  AssignmentInfo assignment = 4;

  // Scheduling attempt history.
  SchedulingInfo scheduling_info = 5;

  // Name/UID of the underlying Sandbox/SandboxClaim object, for debugging.
  // AgentJob's own phase is authoritative; this is a disposable implementation
  // detail, not a second source of truth for terminal state.
  string sandbox_ref = 6;

  // Structured result, populated once the harness reports a result.
  AgentJobResult result = 7;

  repeated Condition status_conditions = 8;
}

message AgentJob {
  option (michelangelo.api.resource) = { conversion: true };

  k8s.io.apimachinery.pkg.apis.meta.v1.TypeMeta type_meta = 1;
  k8s.io.apimachinery.pkg.apis.meta.v1.ObjectMeta metadata = 2;

  AgentJobSpec spec = 3;
  AgentJobStatus status = 4;
}

message AgentJobList {
  option (michelangelo.api.resource_list) = {};

  k8s.io.apimachinery.pkg.apis.meta.v1.TypeMeta type_meta = 1;
  k8s.io.apimachinery.pkg.apis.meta.v1.ListMeta metadata = 2;

  repeated AgentJob items = 3;
}
Notes on reuse vs. new fields: TerminationSpec/TerminationType (job.proto) are deliberately not reused for AgentJob's terminal reporting, because their 2-value SUCCEEDED/FAILED enum can't represent the infra/app failure distinction this RFC treats as a core goal; AgentJobPhase and AgentJobFailureReason exist specifically to carry that extra granularity, while still reusing Affinity, SchedulingSpec, AssignmentInfo, SchedulingInfo, UserInfo, ClusterSpec verbatim wherever their existing semantics already match.

The CRUD/List service (agent_job_svc.proto) is generated, not hand-authored, via tools/grpc-svc-gen.sh AgentJob, following the same pattern as every other Michelangelo resource.

Foreign-CRD reference gap
Michelangelo's existing (michelangelo.api.resource_reference) proto annotation (used e.g. by SparkJobSpec's cluster reference) only supports referencing another Michelangelo-native CRD by its michelangelo.uber.com/<Kind> type string. There is no existing annotation for referencing a foreign CRD (like agent-sandbox's Sandbox/SandboxClaim). This RFC proposes sandbox_ref as a plain string field with no cross-reference validation for the initial version, and flags extending michelangelo/api/options.proto with a foreign-CRD-reference annotation as an open question rather than a blocking requirement (see Open Questions).

Alternatives considered
Alternative A: Plain batch/v1.Job, no AgentJob CRD, no sandbox integration
Pros: Zero new API surface; teams already do this today by hand. Cons: No Michelangelo scheduling integration (resource pools, Affinity, RBAC via UserInfo), no consistent phase/status model, and — most importantly — no strong isolation guarantee. Why not chosen: Leaves the isolation problem this RFC exists to solve entirely unsolved.

Alternative B: Extend Job (the existing generic Michelangelo job CRD) with an "agent mode"
Pros: No new CRD to register/maintain. Cons: Job's existing semantics aren't built around a payload-driven, single-shot, sandbox-backed execution model with a richer failure taxonomy; overloading it would require enough new optional fields that the result is effectively a new CRD anyway, just without a clean name. Why not chosen: Consistent with how RayJob/SparkJob were split out from generic Job rather than folded into it — Michelangelo's own precedent favors a dedicated CRD per compute engine over one increasingly-overloaded generic Job.

Alternative C: Reimplement pod-level isolation directly instead of depending on kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox
Pros: No external dependency to track or vendor. Cons: Duplicates real, already-solved isolation engineering (gVisor RuntimeClass setup, warm-pool management, exec API) that agent-sandbox already provides and maintains upstream. Why not chosen: No OSS job-CRD project surveyed (Argo, Flyte, RayJob) reimplements sandboxing once a dedicated isolation primitive exists in the ecosystem — depending on agent-sandbox is the whole point of using it instead of reinventing it.

Alternative D: Model AgentJob as a Uniflow pipeline-task plugin (à la RayTask), not a standalone CRD
Pros: Matches Michelangelo's existing lighter-weight "task owns the resource" pattern for adding a new compute backend to a pipeline; avoids a new top-level CRD, gRPC service, RBAC surface, and CRD versioning/backward-compatibility obligations under go/api/crd/sync.go. Cons: Makes AgentJob invisible and unmanageable outside the pipeline that launched it — no independent get/list, no direct submission by a non-pipeline caller, no standalone audit trail. Why not chosen: Agent-harness runs are expected to be submitted and polled independently at least as often as RayJob/SparkJob are, and per Michelangelo's own job-CRD architecture, job CRDs are typically launched by a workflow activity but still exist as independently addressable resources — the heavier CRD route is justified. A future go/worker/activities/agent + workflows/agent pair (out of scope here, see Non-goals) can still let a pipeline launch an AgentJob the same way it launches a RayJob today.

Open questions
 Owner references vs. finalizer for Sandbox/SandboxClaim cleanup — does agent-sandbox support Kubernetes owner references from a foreign apiGroup (Michelangelo's AgentJob) cleanly, or does this RFC need an explicit finalizer-driven delete instead? This is safety-critical (leaked sandboxes are leaked, potentially security-sensitive compute) and must be resolved before implementation, not deferred.
 Sandbox/SandboxClaim pooling model — does agent-sandbox's Sandbox/SandboxClaim distinction map 1:1 onto one-sandbox-per-AgentJob, or is there a claim-reuse/pooling model that complicates the "who owns/deletes what" story above?
 Foreign-CRD reference annotation — should michelangelo/api/options.proto be extended with a (michelangelo.api.foreign_resource_reference)-style annotation for referencing non-Michelangelo CRDs like Sandbox, or is a plain unvalidated string field (as proposed above for sandbox_ref) acceptable for the initial version?
 Concrete CI-gated integration/e2e test. The repo currently has no codified Go e2e test target for controllers managing a foreign CRD (existing "sandbox integration tests" are a manually-invoked poetry run ma sandbox create step, not a named CI target). This RFC should not merge with "will add sandbox tests" left unresolved — a concrete scenario (submit AgentJob → Sandbox/SandboxClaim created → harness runs → terminal phase observed → resources GC'd) and its CI target name should be agreed before implementation begins.
 Single- vs multi-version CRD at launch. go/api/crd/sync.go's SyncCRDs rejects backward-incompatible schema changes by default once a CRD has live instances. Should AgentJob launch single-version (simpler, faster early iteration) and defer multi-version support until the schema stabilizes?
 Vendored agent-sandbox API version. Which exact module path/version of kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox's Go API types should be vendored and pinned (mirroring how github.com/ray-project/kuberay/ray-operator/apis/ray/v1 is vendored today), given agent-sandbox is itself an early-stage SIG project with no long-term API stability guarantee yet?
 CLI/SDK verbs beyond get/apply/delete. Should AgentJob ship a logs/watch/run CLI verb at launch, given RayJob/SparkJob don't have these today and it's a documented DX gap? Given "sandboxed, run-to-completion" workloads are exactly where users most want to tail output and debug failures, is inheriting this gap acceptable for v1?
 Should payload have a size cap enforced at the API layer, given it's stored inline as a google.protobuf.Struct rather than a reference to external storage?
Rollout strategy
Land agent_job.proto/agent_job_svc.proto first (API surface only, no controller): write the proto, run tools/grpc-svc-gen.sh AgentJob, add both files to proto/api/v2/BUILD.bazel's v2_proto.srcs, run tools/gazelle, bazel build //proto/..., then tools/gen-proto-go.sh to sync proto-go/. Launch as a single-version CRD (see Open Questions) to avoid engaging crd.SyncCRDs's backward-incompatible-change rejection during early iteration.
Implement the go/components/agentjob controller behind a feature flag:
Registration mirrors go/components/ray/job/module.go exactly: NewReconciler(...) constructor, Register(mgr) error, wired via fx.Invoke(register).
Watch strategy: .For(&v2pb.AgentJob{}).Owns(&sandboxv1.Sandbox{}) — explicitly not the poll-based approach RayJob uses for its federated (remote-cluster) RayJob, since Sandbox/SandboxClaim are same-cluster.
Add the corresponding RBAC ClusterRole diff (helm/michelangelo/templates/rbac/clusterrole.yaml) for agent-sandbox's actual apiGroup/resources (sandboxes, sandboxclaims, and their /status subresources) in the same PR as the controller — this is hand-maintained YAML, not generated, and easy to silently omit.
Differentiated requeue/backoff for provisioning vs. running states, rather than a single flat interval, given sandbox cold-start latency variance.
Target a single sandbox cluster for initial validation before enabling across all ClusterSpec targets.
Start with runtime variant 1 (harness-inside-sandbox) only; document variant 2 as a future extension (see Open Questions).
Ship the closed loop of artifacts in one PR: proto + generated service proto + Bazel BUILD wiring
generated proto-go/ sources + RBAC diff + fx module wiring + unit tests (fake client + gomock, table-driven, covering every reconcile branch, mirroring go/components/ray/job/controller_test.go)
the named CI-gated integration test agreed in Open Questions.
No migration path needed — this is wholly new API surface with no prior AgentJob resource to migrate from.
